What is sshd_config?

It’s the configuration file for the OpenSSH daemon (sshd).

Location (default):

/etc/ssh/sshd_config


This file is read automatically when the SSH service starts (or restarts).

Every Linux distro that ships with OpenSSH (Ubuntu, RHEL, CentOS, Debian, Amazon Linux, etc.) will have it by default.

🔹 What does it control?

It’s not an extra package — it’s part of the SSH server itself.

Who can log in (users, groups).

Authentication methods (password, public key, certificates, MFA).

Whether root can log in.

Which commands a user is forced to run.

Network features (port forwarding, X11, tunneling).

Chroot jails.

Session timeouts.

